[Page:Loading]
SectionPrefix=Loading

[Loading:decryptor:The game arrives encrypted]
The five code blocks on the tape cannot be disassembled as they stand. The BASIC loader is
<pre>10 BORDER 0: CLEAR 24574: FOR q=2 TO 6: LOAD "AticAtac"+STR$ q CODE
   : NEXT q: PRINT USR 23424</pre>
which loads a screen at 0x4000, the game itself at 0x5FFF, and three blocks so small they look like mistakes: eighteen bytes at 0x5B80, one byte at 0x5CB0 and two bytes at 0x5C78. Then it starts execution at 23424 &mdash; which is 0x5B80, the printer buffer, not the game.
What is sitting there is a decryptor:
<pre>5B80: 21 FF 5F   LD HL,$5FFF
5B83: 01 7C 00   LD BC,$007C     ; 124 x 256 = 31744 bytes
5B86: AF         XOR A
5B87: ED 67      RRD             ; chained nibble-rotate through A
5B89: 23         INC HL
5B8A: 10 FB      DJNZ $5B87
5B8C: 0D         DEC C
5B8D: 20 F8      JR NZ,$5B87
5B8F: C3 00 60   JP $6000</pre>
<code>RRD</code> rotates one nibble out of <code>(HL)</code> into <code>A</code> and <code>A</code>'s low nibble into <code>(HL)</code>, so a single nibble is dragged through the entire block. Every byte depends on the one before it, and the plaintext exists only in RAM &mdash; there is no offline transformation that recovers it. The tape has to be run.

[Loading:frames:The second lock, in the frame counter]
The two bytes at 0x5C78 land on FRAMES, the ROM's frame counter, and the value poked in is 0x255E. That is not arbitrary: it is the first thing the decrypted code checks.
<pre>6000: F3         DI
6001: 31 00 5E   LD SP,$5E00
6004: 3A 79 5C   LD A,($5C79)    ; FRAMES+1
6007: FE 25      CP $25          ; ...must still be $25
6009: C0         RET NZ          ; or fall straight back to BASIC
600A: C3 19 7C   JP $7C19        ; the real entry point</pre>
A loader that drops the little blocks, or that lets an interrupt tick FRAMES on before arriving here, gets a game that returns quietly to BASIC with no error at all.

[Loading:trampoline:The one-byte block is an instruction]
The single byte at 0x5CB0 is the neatest of the three. Its value is 0xE9, the opcode for <code>JP (HL)</code>, and the game jumps to it every time it dispatches an actor. The indirect jump at the heart of the main loop is an instruction that exists only because the loader poked it into spare system-variable space.

[Page:Architecture]
SectionPrefix=Architecture

[Architecture:records:One record shape for everything]
The player and every monster share one record. The first eight bytes describe the thing itself and mean the same for both; what the second eight hold depends on which kind it is.
<table class="default">
<tr><th>Offset</th><th>Every record</th></tr>
<tr><td>+$00</td><td>sprite, which also selects the handler</td></tr>
<tr><td>+$01</td><td>room</td></tr>
<tr><td>+$02</td><td>a flag</td></tr>
<tr><td>+$03, +$04</td><td>x and y &mdash; the point its feet stand on, since sprites are drawn upwards</td></tr>
<tr><td>+$05</td><td>drawing mode: top three bits pick the drawing routine, low two how the sprite combines with the screen, bit 6 a doorway's facing</td></tr>
</table>
For a monster the rest is how it moves &mdash; +$08 and +$09 are x and y velocity, nudged one step at a time towards a limit of plus or minus 2, and +$0D and +$0F are countdowns. For the player the rest is the weapon it has in flight: +$08 its type ($00 when there is nothing in the air), +$09 its room, +$0B and +$0C its position and +$0E its signed velocity. The same offsets, read two different ways depending on whose record it is.
Doors, the title screen's pictures and the scratch records the interface is drawn from are eight bytes only &mdash; the common header and nothing after it.
The player's record is at 0xEA90 and the monsters occupy eight from 0xEE60 to 0xEED0. None of it appears in this disassembly: it is all above the loaded block, so it is runtime state.

[Architecture:mainloop:One loop, three tables]
#R$7DC3 is the whole of the game's structure. It walks three tables of records and hands each to the dispatcher; nothing sits above it.
<table class="default">
<tr><th>Table</th><th>Record</th><th>What is in it</th><th>Dispatched</th></tr>
<tr><td>$EA90-$EAA7</td><td>8 bytes</td><td>the player, its weapon, the sound slot</td><td>once per frame, by #R$7EB2</td></tr>
<tr><td>$EAA8-$EE5F</td><td>8 bytes</td><td>objects, collectables, sounds</td><td>only if in the player's room</td></tr>
<tr><td>$EE60-$EEDF</td><td>16 bytes</td><td>the monsters</td><td>every pass, wherever they are</td></tr>
<tr><td>$EEE0 up</td><td>8 bytes</td><td>doors</td><td>only if in the player's room</td></tr>
</table>
The first of those four is not walked by the loop itself. #R$7EB2 does it, and only when the ROM's frame counter has moved on, so the player and its weapon are updated at a steady 50Hz while everything below them is dispatched as fast as the loop can go. A crowded room slows the monsters down; it does not slow the player.
Monsters being dispatched without a room test is what keeps the castle alive behind you: they go on moving through rooms you cannot see, and are where you left them when you come back.
Two instructions at the top of the loop carry more weight than their size suggests. <code>LD SP,$5E00</code> resets the stack on every pass, so no handler has to leave it as it found it &mdash; which is what lets a routine like #R$8A15 abandon its caller and jump straight to the death routine when the life force runs out. And the <code>EI</code> beside it is where the <code>DI</code> from the entry point is finally lifted; everything before this runs with interrupts off.
The dispatch itself is a jump, not a call. The loop puts the address to come back to in HL, and #R$7E7E pushes it before jumping to the handler, so the handler's own RET lands back in the loop.

[Architecture:dispatch:There is no switch statement]
Each actor's sprite byte is an index into a 202-entry table of handler addresses, and #R$7E7E jumps to whichever it finds:
<pre>LD HL,ACTOR_HANDLERS
LD C,(IX+$00)      ; the sprite byte doubles as the type
SLA C / RL B       ; two bytes per entry
ADD HL,BC
LD A,(HL) / INC HL / LD H,(HL) / LD L,A
JP $5CB0           ; the JP (HL) the tape poked into place</pre>
That is why so many of the per-creature routines have nothing at all referencing them: they are reachable only through a table which, to a disassembler, is data.

[Architecture:types:What the sprite codes mean]
Entries run in groups, because the low bits of the sprite byte are the animation frame rather than part of the identity. The first three groups are the playable characters, sixteen codes each:
<table class="default">
<tr><th>Sprite codes</th><th>Handler</th><th>What it is</th></tr>
<tr><td>$01-$10</td><td>#R$8E26</td><td>the knight</td></tr>
<tr><td>$11-$20</td><td>#R$80D2</td><td>the wizard</td></tr>
<tr><td>$21-$30</td><td>#R$8DC4</td><td>the serf</td></tr>
<tr><td>$4C-$4D</td><td>#R$845F</td><td>the pumpkin</td></tr>
<tr><td>$5C-$5D</td><td>#R$845F</td><td>the spider &mdash; the same routine, a different creature</td></tr>
<tr><td>$66, $67</td><td>#R$8CB7, #R$8D45</td><td>the player rising and sinking</td></tr>
</table>
This is what makes the threshold in #R$85B2 meaningful: codes below $31 are exactly the three playable characters, so "non-zero and below $31" is a test for "the player is currently in play". Dying does not move or hide the player &mdash; it changes this one byte to $67, and the dispatcher does the rest, sinking them into the floor and raising them again as $66. Both are outside the player band, so the same test that finds a player also gives the invulnerability while they sink and rise, without a line of code anywhere saying so.

[Architecture:roomcontents:What is in a room]
A room's furniture is not searched for. #R$757D holds one pointer per room to a $0000-terminated list of the records that belong to it, and #R$902B walks that list when the room is set up. Room $00's list names $EEE0, $EEF0 and $EF00, which are the three door records a running game really does have there.
The addresses in the lists are stored with $757D added to them, so each has to have it taken off again. That looks like obfuscation and is not: BC still holds $757D from the lookup two instructions earlier, so both the bias and its removal are free. It is the same habit as the tile source at $5E01 and the sprite bases at $A600 &mdash; a pointer's base made to carry information rather than a separate field doing it.

[Architecture:doors:A door is two records]
Every door exists twice, once in each room it joins, and the two halves are put eight bytes apart on purpose. Finding the far side is then one instruction:
<pre>LD A,L / XOR $08 / LD L,A     ; IX now points at the other half</pre>
No pointer and no lookup. Each half carries the destination room and the position you arrive at going that way, so stepping through a door means reading the half you did <em>not</em> touch &mdash; which is the first thing #R$9117 does.
The open-or-shut state is bit 3 of the drawing mode, and #R$954D and #R$9565 always set or clear it on both halves at once, so the two sides can never disagree. A locked door is not a separate kind of thing: it is an ordinary door whose handler shuts it again unless the player is carrying a key of the matching colour.

[Architecture:characterdoors:Doors that know who you are]
Three of the doors will only open for one character: sprite $B2 for the knight, $B9 for the wizard and $BC for the serf. The test costs four instructions and no extra state, because a character's identity is already in the player's sprite byte -- each owns sixteen consecutive codes, so subtracting a character's first code and asking whether what is left is under $10 answers "is the player this one".
<pre>LD A,($EA90)     ; the player's sprite
SUB $21          ; the serf's first code -- or $11, or 1
CP $10           ; sixteen codes per character
JR NC,shut</pre>
Fail it and the door is still drawn; it simply does not open. The same three lines with a different constant give three different doors, which is the whole implementation.

[Architecture:controls:Controls]
#R$93BE returns one byte for all three control methods, in Kempston's bit order &mdash; bit 0 right, 1 left, 2 down, 3 up, 4 fire, a 0 bit meaning pressed. The keyboard needs its bits shuffled to get there, because Q and W sit in the opposite order to left and right, and that shuffle is what lets the movement code be written once instead of three times. The keys are Q left, W right, E down, R up and T fire.

[Architecture:method:How this was worked out]
The code/data split is not a heuristic. The build plays the game in SkoolKit's Z80 simulator, once per character and control method, and records the address of every instruction actually executed; anything it never reached stays marked as data, so unreached code appears as DEFBs rather than as invented instructions.
That leaves a gap, and it is worth knowing how it is closed. Code the playthroughs never ran stays marked as data -- the collectables handler at #R$92F5 is one, because mashing keys at random never walks the player onto an object. But those routines are not unreachable: they are entries in ACTOR_HANDLERS or in one of the sprite-drawer tables, which is proof they are code even though nothing executed them. Checking every entry in those tables against the block map found fifteen such addresses, and declaring them code recovered about 550 bytes of readable disassembly. The declaration is not taken on trust: if any one of them were not really code, the round trip below would stop reassembling the game byte for byte.
The comments were then checked against a running emulator &mdash; breaking on a routine and reading its registers and memory back &mdash; which is how the record layout, the handler table and the key map above were each confirmed rather than guessed.
As a correctness check the generated assembly is fed back through sjasmplus and compared against the decrypted memory it came from. All 30208 bytes match byte for byte, and the build fails if they ever stop matching.

[Architecture:orientations:Eight ways to draw a sprite]
There are eight drawing routines in each of the two families, and the reason is that a sprite is only ever stored once. Four of them are the four ways a set of bytes can be put on the screen &mdash; as stored, mirrored left to right, turned upside down, or both &mdash; and the top three bits of an actor's +$05 choose between them.
Mirroring costs two changes to the copying loop: the row is walked with <code>DEC DE</code> instead of <code>INC DE</code>, and every byte passes through #R$9A92, which rolls its eight bits out of one register and into another in the opposite order. Turning a sprite over costs one call before the loop starts, to move the data pointer to the last row.
So a creature that faces four ways is one set of bytes and a number in +$05. It is the same economy as the room outlines, where three shapes share an edge list and differ only in their vertices.

[Architecture:sounds:Sounds are creatures too]
There is no sound scheduler. A sound effect is spawned into a record like any other actor, with one of the sprite codes that draws nothing -- $64, $65 or $A0 -- and a frame count where a creature would keep its room number. The dispatcher then finds it every frame and calls its handler, which beeps once, counts down, and zeroes its own sprite when it reaches nothing, at which point the dispatcher stops finding it.
That is what lets a note slide: each handler works its pitch out from however much of the countdown is left, so a sound sweeps over the frames it lasts without anything having to remember where it had got to.
It also explains why the handler table has entries that draw no picture. They are not gaps -- they are sounds.

[Architecture:selfmodifying:The game writes its own instructions]
Four separate places assemble an instruction at run time rather than branching, and they are four different tricks rather than one habit applied four times.
<table class="default">
<tr><th>Where</th><th>What is written</th><th>Why</th></tr>
<tr><td>#R$96AF</td><td>the operand of SET b,(HL)</td><td>the bit number is a room number, not known until it runs</td></tr>
<tr><td>#R$9C2F</td><td>the displacement in LD r,(IX+d)</td><td>the vertex index comes out of the room's edge list</td></tr>
<tr><td>#R$99C9</td><td>NOP, OR (HL), XOR (HL) or AND (HL)</td><td>how a sprite meets the screen, decided once instead of per byte</td></tr>
<tr><td>#R$9F9F</td><td>the displacement of a JR</td><td>picks how far down an unrolled chain of shifts to enter, which positions a sprite to the pixel</td></tr>
</table>
The last is the neatest. A sprite is two bytes wide but lands at a pixel x, so it has to be shifted; instead of storing eight pre-shifted copies or running a loop with a counter, #R$9F2B holds seven copies of ADD HL,HL / ADC A,A and the patched jump lands part-way down it. Entering n pairs in performs 7 - n shifts, with no counter and no branch.

[Page:Data]
SectionPrefix=Data

[Data:intro:Every byte accounted for]
The game is 30208 bytes and about two thirds of it is not code. This page is
what that two thirds turned out to be. Nothing here was guessed from a hex
dump: every table was found by following the code that reads it, and every
extent was checked by seeing whether it tiled its region without leaving a
byte over.

[Data:template:The castle, written out in full]
5488 bytes at #R$600D are simply the whole runtime area -- $EA90 upwards --
as it looks before the game starts, copied into place with one LDIR. It holds
three empty records for the player, the weapon and the sound slot; 115 objects,
among them sixteen mushrooms and ten each of six kinds of food; five monsters,
one each of the mummy, Dracula, the devil, Frankenstein's monster and the
humpback; and 274 doors.
The doors are sixteen bytes each, not eight -- one record holding both sides.
The room lists prove it: of the 274, exactly 272 are named by two different
rooms, which is what a door joining two rooms looks like from the data. That
is also why DOOR_OTHER_SIDE flips bit 3 of an address rather than adding
anything: it is moving between the two halves of one record.
Three routines edit this template before it is copied, choosing rooms out of
small tables using the frame counter. So the key pieces and a few other things
are somewhere different every game, and the randomness is applied to the
picture of the castle rather than to the castle itself.

[Data:graphics:The sprites]
Every sprite's address is in #R$A4BE, 239 entries of two bytes. The graphics
themselves carry no length: the first byte is the row count and every sprite is
two bytes -- sixteen pixels -- wide.
That was not read off a hex dump either. Each code was drawn on a machine of its
own with the reads into the graphics region logged, and all 235 that draw read
exactly one plus twice the row count, contiguously, from the address the table
holds. The fourteen graphics below $AD2E are the wider pieces the rooms are
built from and carry a width as well, and those fourteen tile their region
exactly, ending on the first byte of ROOM_TABLE.
A few hundred bytes scattered through the region are pictures that nothing
points at and nothing reads -- artwork that did not make it into the finished
game.

[Data:outlines:The rooms]
A room is a shape number and a colour. The shape gives how far the player may
walk from the centre each way, a table of corners, and a list of which corners
to join up. There are thirteen shapes; three of them -- $06, $07 and $0C --
are complete, with corners and edges of their own, and no room uses any of them.
What is in a room is a separate list, and those lists follow their index with no
gaps at all, filling everything up to $7C18, the byte before the game's own
entry point.

[Data:fonts:The two character sets]
There are two. The text font at $BF4C has 59 characters, $20 to $5A, so the
game can print spaces, digits, punctuation and capitals and nothing else. The
code never names that address: it loads $BE4C, which is the same block less $20
characters, so a character's own code indexes it, and $BFCC, one bias further
on, so that a digit's value indexes its own glyph.
The title screen uses a second set of 94 characters and a 192-byte map of which
piece goes in each of its 8 by 24 cells -- the picture is a little font, not a
bitmap.

[Data:typo:CONGRATULATIONT]
The message shown when the player escapes reads CONGRATULATION and then $D4,
which is "T" with the end marker set. Drawing the screen confirms it: the game
says CONGRATULATIONT. The sequence that plays it -- a note climbing while the
screen flashes in and out from the middle -- is never reached by a machine
playing the game at random, which is why it sat in the disassembly as data
until it was picked apart by hand.
